---

<BaseLayout current="blog" title="Why I do what I do · Pradyut Kumar">
  <article>
    <h1>Why I do what I do</h1>

    <p class="article-meta">
      <time datetime={published}>March 18, 2026</time> · 4 min read
    </p>

    <p>
      You know that scene in <em>Avengers: Infinity War</em> where Doctor Strange scrolls
      through 14,000,605 possible futures to find the one where the Avengers win? That's
      roughly how I think about mathematical modelling — a tool for peeking at countless
      possible outcomes and picking the best response before you commit to one in real life.
    </p>

    <p>
      Below is a small SEIR simulator you can poke at. Drag the sliders to see how the shape
      of an outbreak changes when R<sub>0</sub> goes up, when people are infectious for longer,
      or when the latent period stretches. It's a toy — the real models I work with have a
      few more moving parts — but it captures the intuition.
    </p>

    <SEIRSimulator />

    <p>
      That kind of predictive power is what excites me: whether it's understanding how a
      disease will spread, which intervention might work best, or how resistance to drugs
      evolves over time. The rest of my research is basically variations on this theme,
      with more realism and more caveats.
    </p>

    <p style="margin-top:2.5rem;">
      <a href="/blog">← back to posts</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
